% ============================================================================
% measuring-tool-si-unit — WHICH internationally-standardized SI unit a lab
% measuring tool's own readings are expressed in, DERIVED (not just looked up)
% by composing a specific CHEMISTRY fact with a general METROLOGY principle
% (adj-facts-stdlib, CHEMISTRY, cross-directory composition with METROLOGY).
% Grounds the "observation and measurement" Major Gap ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md
% §5.1 names for K-8 science.
% ============================================================================
% `chemistry/measuring-tools.adj` already tables WHICH quantity each common lab
% tool measures ("a ruler measures length, a thermometer measures temperature");
% `metrology/si-base-units.adj` already tables what the ONE internationally
% agreed SI unit for a GIVEN base quantity IS ("length's base unit is the
% meter, temperature's base unit is the kelvin"). Neither file states outright
% "a thermometer's readings are standardized in kelvin" — but measuring-tools'
% specific tool→quantity assignment, run through si-base-units' general
% metrology PRINCIPLE for that quantity, answers it directly: this is the SAME
% "compose two independently-citable facts into a derived, dual-cited
% conclusion" discipline `physics/heat-causes-phase-change.adj`,
% `physics/force-causes-acceleration.adj`, and
% `geology/earth-layer-matter-behavior.adj` already established, applied here
% to a genuinely CROSS-DIRECTORY pair (chemistry + metrology, not two files in
% the same subject directory) — the same cross-directory SHAPE
% `earth-layer-matter-behavior.adj` proved out (its `has_state` plays the role
% this file's `measuring_tool` plays; its `matter_state` plays the role this
% file's `si_base_unit` plays), applied here to the OBSERVATION/MEASUREMENT
% gap rather than an Earth-processes one.
%
%     import "measuring-tool-si-unit.adj"        % transitively imports measuring-tools.adj + ../metrology/si-base-units.adj
%     ? measuring_tool_si_unit(thermometer, $Unit, $Symbol)   % kelvin, "K"
%     ? measuring_tool_si_unit(ruler, $Unit, $Symbol)         % meter, "m"
%     ? measuring_tool_si_unit(balance, $Unit, $Symbol)       % kilogram, "kg"
%     ? measuring_tool_si_unit($T, kelvin, $Symbol)           % thermometer (reverse)
%     ? measuring_tool_si_unit(graduated_cylinder, $U, $S)    % abstain -- volume is a DERIVED SI unit, not one of the 7 BASE units
%
% This is a native `rule` (ADJ-TABLES RS-6): its body binds `$Tool`/`$Quantity`
% through the already-shipped `measuring_tool` table, then re-binds
% `$Quantity`/`$Unit`/`$Symbol` through the already-shipped `si_base_unit`
% table -- a plain two-hop join on the literal, EXACT quantity word both
% tables share. A query's `steps` trail therefore shows THREE entries: the
% rule's own citation (the general SI-standardization principle), the
% `measuring_tool` fact's citation (Chemistry LibreTexts), and the
% `si_base_unit` fact's citation (NIST), so a human can independently verify
% each link.
%
% WHY this is a genuine derivation, not a coincidental word match: a tool's
% raw reading is only USEFUL to another scientist once it is tied to the one
% unit everyone has agreed to measure that quantity in -- that is the entire
% point of the SI system, and it is exactly the link `measuring_tools.adj`'s
% own header names as the gap it fills ("observation and measurement") without
% yet closing the loop to a standardized unit. This rule closes that loop: a
% ruler's centimeters and a thermometer's degrees Celsius are both informal
% everyday framings of the SAME base quantities (length, temperature) whose
% ONE internationally-standardized unit is what `si_base_unit` tables, making
% "which SI unit does this tool's reading reduce to" a first-class, queryable,
% dual-cited fact instead of background intuition a learner is expected to
% already have.
%
% HONEST ABSTENTION -- the crux of why this is a narrow, faithful composition
% and not a forced one: `measuring_tool` tables FOUR tools, but `si_base_unit`
% only defines the SEVEN quantities SI calls "base" (length, mass, time,
% electric_current, temperature, amount_of_substance, luminous_intensity).
% Three of the four tools join cleanly:
%
%     tool                  measuring_tool's quantity   matches si_base_unit's key?   SI unit
%     -------------------   --------------------------  ----------------------------  ---------
%     ruler                 length                       YES (length)                  meter
%     balance                mass                         YES (mass)                    kilogram
%     thermometer            temperature                  YES (temperature)             kelvin
%     graduated_cylinder      volume                       NO -- volume is DERIVED       ABSTAIN
%
% `metrology/si-derived-units.adj` (already shipped, in this SAME package) is
% the independent confirmation of WHY: that sibling table's own header
% explains that volume (`si_derived_unit(volume, "m3")`) is "one rung up from
% the seven base units" -- a quantity built by RAISING the base unit meter to
% the third power, not a base quantity in its own right. Forcing
% `graduated_cylinder` to join against `si_base_unit` anyway (e.g. by
% asserting the base unit `meter` is "the" SI unit for volume) would conflate
% a base quantity with a derived one -- a real metrology distinction the
% cited NIST source itself draws, and exactly the kind of invented mapping
% this stdlib's honest-abstention discipline forbids. So this rule derives
% exactly the three tools whose own tabled quantity is a literal, exact match
% for one of SI's seven keyed BASE quantities, and abstains on the fourth,
% rather than inventing a base-unit assignment for a derived quantity.
%
% CROSS-DIRECTORY IMPORT: this file lives in `chemistry/` (its natural
% subject home, alongside `measuring-tools.adj`, the specific-instance table)
% and imports its metrology sibling with a relative
% `../metrology/si-base-units.adj` path -- empirically confirmed (in a
% scratch dir, before writing this file) against the real built CLI binary,
% mirroring `earth-layer-matter-behavior.adj`'s own confirmed cross-directory
% shape. This library's OWN `.query.adj` companion is placed at the package
% root (not beside this file), the same placement
% `earth-layer-matter-behavior.query.adj` already established, so a bare
% `adj-lang-cli <query>.query.adj` invocation from the package root works
% directly.
%
% Deliberately scoped to the four lab tools `measuring-tools.adj` itself
% already commits to (no weather or ocean instruments -- those are DIFFERENT
% already-shipped "observation and measurement" axes, `meteorology/
% weather-instruments.adj` and `oceanography/ocean-observing-instruments.adj`,
% whose own quantity vocabulary -- wind_speed, atmospheric_pressure,
% underwater_sound, etc. -- does not literally match any of `si_base_unit`'s
% seven keyed quantities, so composing either of those tables here would
% either produce zero rows or require inventing an unstated mapping) and to
% the seven everyday base quantities `si-base-units.adj` itself already
% commits to -- no new tool or unit content is asserted here, only the join.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "measuring-tools.adj"
import "../metrology/si-base-units.adj"

rule { head: measuring_tool_si_unit($Tool, $Unit, $Symbol)
       when: measuring_tool($Tool, $Quantity), si_base_unit($Quantity, $Unit, $Symbol)
       source "The SI is made up of 7 base units that define the 22 derived units with special names and symbols."
       locator "https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si/si-units"
       trust authoritative }
